6. Probing framework
6.1 Two planes.
• Underlay plane describes the ISP path. Probes MUST bypass the tunnel and use the physical network under assessment: obtain its Network from NetworkMonitor and use Network.getAllByName() for DNS and Network.getSocketFactory(), Network.bindSocket() or Network.openConnection() for TCP, TLS and HTTP. Verify on a device, or with debug logging plus documented reasoning, that these probes do not enter the TUN. If the routing makes this impossible, record it in the audit and cap confidence accordingly. Never use bindProcessToNetwork for this.
• Overlay plane describes tunnel health. Probes traverse the tunnel exactly like ordinary app traffic, or go through the core’s measurement API.
• Never mix the planes: a failed overlay probe says nothing about the underlay, and a successful underlay probe never proves the tunnel works.
6.2 Probe layers. Every result records its layer and outcome.
• L-IP: TCP connect to an IP literal (no DNS). It separates DNS problems from IP blocking.
• L-DNS: resolve a registry hostname through the network’s resolver and classify the answer: public address, non-public address (private, loopback, link-local, CGNAT), empty, or error. A non-public answer for a public hostname signals DNS_TAMPERING_SUSPECTED and is never used as a connect target.
• L-TLS: handshake with SNI using the platform trust store and hostname verification. Distinguish timeout, reset after ClientHello, alert and certificate failure.
• L-HTTP: minimal HTTPS request (HEAD or GET to a 204 or tiny resource). Validate the status and the expected length or body marker. An unexpected redirect or body signals CAPTIVE_PORTAL_SUSPECTED or HTTP_BLOCKPAGE_SUSPECTED.
• L-XFER: bounded transfer (default 256 KB) that measures throughput and mid-transfer stalls; budgeted, and used only to separate partial or throttled conditions from normal.
Reuse the project’s HTTP client. If OkHttp is already present, its EventListener provides per-phase timings; otherwise use platform HttpsURLConnection and SSLSocket. Do not add a dependency for this.
6.3 Probe Endpoint Registry.
• Data-driven: an asset file (assets/connectivity/probe_endpoints.json) plus operator and user overrides in DataStore. Fields: id, role (INTERNATIONAL, DOMESTIC, RELAY_CHECK, TUNNEL_CHECK), host, port, ipLiterals, path, expectedStatus, optional expected length or body marker, providerGroup, weight, enabled.
• International defaults: only endpoints published for connectivity checking (the public 204-style captive-portal check URLs used by major platforms), from at least three independent provider groups, plus the developer’s own endpoint if configured. List the shipped defaults in the final report; they must be replaceable without a code change.
• Domestic defaults: empty. Domestic endpoints are added only by the operator or user (for example an endpoint on the operator’s own domestic-reachable host). Do not ship any government, bank, commercial or third-party-app endpoint.
• Tunnel-check endpoints (overlay): the developer’s own 204 endpoint if configured; otherwise the same public check URLs.
• Validate on load: HTTPS only for HTTP probes, no credentials in URLs, a bounded number of entries per role (default 8), well-formed hosts.
6.4 Execution rules.
• Single-flight: one assessment cycle at a time, triggers coalesced. At most 3 probes in parallel. Hard cycle budget (default 10 s). Each layer has its own timeout, enforced with withTimeout and cooperative cancellation.
• Independence: conclusions about «international» as a whole require at least 2 distinct providerGroups. With fewer, cap confidence at 0.6 and say so in the evidence.
• Minimum interval between full cycles (default 5 s); results stay valid for a TTL (default 20 s) unless the network changes.
6.5 Budgets. At most 32 KB per cycle excluding L-XFER. L-XFER at most once per 30 minutes, and not on metered networks unless the planner needs it and the user allows it. Daily probe-data cap on metered networks (default 2 MB). Screen off: minimal cadence (default 5 min). A low-data mode in settings halves the cadence and disables L-XFER.
6.6 Free signals (supporting evidence only, never a replacement for probes): NetworkCapabilities flags (INTERNET, VALIDATED, CAPTIVE_PORTAL, NOT_METERED), onBlockedStatusChanged, the background-data restriction status, and the VALIDATED flag of the VPN network itself as a weak overlay signal.
6.7 Result model (illustrative; follow the project’s conventions):
enum class Plane { UNDERLAY, OVERLAY }
enum class ProbeLayer { IP, DNS, TLS, HTTP, XFER }

