One failed foreign server is never evidence of domestic-only connectivity. Rule 7 can only fire when domestic endpoints were actually configured and tested.
7.4 Confidence. confidence = clamp(coverage × agreement × freshness): coverage is the number of independent provider groups tested divided by 3 (capped at 1), agreement is the share of probes that support the winning hypothesis, and freshness decays with the age of the evidence. «Suspected» states never exceed 0.9. The formula is a pure function with unit tests.
7.5 Hysteresis. A state change requires 2 consecutive consistent cycles and a minimum dwell (default 10 s). Exceptions: NO_NETWORK from OS callbacks, and the return to NORMAL_INTERNATIONAL after verified tunnel traffic. The UI shows the stable state, not raw cycle results.
7.6 Output and triggers. StateFlow<NetworkAssessment> with state, confidence, transport kind, domestic and international reachability, timestamp and sanitized evidence (probe IDs and outcomes, never user server addresses). Triggers: engine start; network change; tunnel-health degradation; adaptive periodic timer; user «recheck». Never from UI recomposition.
8. DomesticReachabilityDiscovery
• Purpose: report whether explicitly configured domestic endpoints are reachable, as evidence for classification and for deciding whether an explicitly configured relay is worth trying. It is not a proxy finder.
• Sources: only operator- or user-configured DOMESTIC registry entries. With none configured the result is NOT_TESTED, not NO.
• Output: DomesticReachability(state = YES | NO | NOT_TESTED, tested, reachable, measuredAt), always reported separately from international transport availability.
• Prohibitions: no third-party domestic service as a relay, tunnel carrier or injection target; no assumption that HTTPS reachability means arbitrary traffic can pass; no inference from DNS alone.
• If a domestic relay is needed, it is an explicitly authorized relay under the operator’s or user’s control (14).
9. Path and server assessment
9.1 ConnectivityPath. The unit of selection is a path, not a server: (profileRef, variant, relayRef?, networkBinding?, trust). Paths are derived only from existing data: user profiles (manual and subscription), user-supplied relays, and, with trust = UNTRUSTED, AutoVpnDiscoveryEngine candidates. Never invent servers, ports or transports that are not in the configuration.
9.2 Assessment ladder (cheapest first; stop at the first failing rung):
1. DNS (consult the per-profile resolved-address cache first).
2. TCP connect to the path’s real address and port.
3. TLS with the path’s real SNI, only where a standalone handshake is meaningful (skip it for protocols whose handshake is not plain TLS and rely on rung 4).
4. Protocol handshake plus real data through the core, using the existing delay or measurement API if the bundled core exposes it, otherwise by bringing the path up as the real tunnel and verifying it.
5. Stability window.
Do not create a second TUN. A measurement-only core instance is allowed only if the bundled library supports it and the audit confirms it does not interfere with the TUN or protect().
9.3 Economy. Assess only the candidates chosen by the planner, at most 5 per recovery round. Success cache TTL 60 s; failure state follows the circuit breaker (11.5).
9.4 Tunnel verification. After connecting, the engine runs at least 2 overlay probes to independent tunnel-check endpoints (1 is allowed when only one is configured, with lower confidence). Success requires the expected status or body and a plausible latency. Then a stability window (default 30 s, at least 3 samples) must pass before the UI may show «اتصال امن برقرار شد»; until then it shows «در حال تأیید اتصال». A started core process is not success and an open TCP port is not success.
9.5 Scoring. Normalized components in [0, 1]: success history (EWMA, half-life 30 min), median latency (log-scaled), consecutive-failure penalty with exponential decay, user-preference bonus (pinned or last-used), trust factor (UNTRUSTED paths are scaled down). Weights live in ConnectivityConfig; the function is pure and unit-tested.
9.6 Privacy. Assessments and logs identify endpoints by endpointId (15.4), never by host, address, UUID or credentials.
10. Failure classification
10.1 Categories. Required: DNS_FAILURE, TCP_TIMEOUT, TCP_REFUSED, TLS_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE, VPN_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE, SERVER_UNREACHABLE, TRANSPORT_UNAVAILABLE, NETWORK_CHANGED, DOMESTIC_ONLY_SUSPECTED, INTERNATIONAL_ROUTE_UNAVAILABLE, RELAY_UNAVAILABLE, AUTHENTICATION_FAILURE, CONFIGURATION_ERROR, UNKNOWN_FAILURE. Strongly recommended additions: TCP_RESET, TLS_CERTIFICATE_INVALID, DNS_TAMPERING_SUSPECTED, CAPTIVE_PORTAL_SUSPECTED, HTTP_BLOCKPAGE_SUSPECTED, THROTTLING_SUSPECTED, CLOCK_SKEW_SUSPECTED, OS_NETWORK_BLOCKED, TUNNEL_NO_TRAFFIC (core running but overlay probes fail).
10.2 Record. FailureRecord: wall-clock timestamp and monotonic time; transport kind; endpointId; category; retry count; recovery action taken; final result (RECOVERED, SWITCHED, GAVE_UP, PENDING); schema version. Never store passwords, private keys, tokens, UUIDs, full configs, subscription URLs or raw server addresses.
10.3 Storage. An in-memory ring buffer (default 200 records) with optional app-private persistence capped by count and age (default 7 days). A user action clears it, and it is excluded from backup.
