12.3 Underlying networks. If the audit shows that the VPN does not declare its underlying networks correctly, call VpnService.setUnderlyingNetworks() with the currently selected physical network whenever it changes, after verifying on a device that this does not regress behavior. Following the system default (null) is acceptable if the audit shows correct behavior.
12.4 Binding Xray’s sockets. Investigate how Xray’s sockets are protected. Acceptable outcomes, in order: (1) sockets follow the system default network through the existing protect() path; (2) per-socket binding with Network.bindSocket() inside the existing protect hook, only if the bridge hands over the descriptor and the result is verified on a device; (3) not supported: report «network selection unavailable» in diagnostics. Do not use bindProcessToNetwork, which is process-wide.
12.5 Multi-network assistance (optional, default OFF). If the active network is assessed RESTRICTED_INTERNATIONAL or DOMESTIC_ONLY_SUSPECTED and another physical network is already available and assessed better, prefer it when 12.4 (2) works; otherwise show a Persian hint. Never keep an extra network alive with requestNetwork, and never use mobile data, without the user’s opt-in.
12.6 Scenarios to cover: Wi-Fi to mobile data; mobile data to Wi-Fi; Wi-Fi with a captive portal; both networks up with one restricted; airplane mode on and off; a flapping network (5 or more events in 10 s) producing no restart storm.
13. Transport resilience
• Inventory from the audit: the transports the bundled Xray build supports AND the config layer can represent. Use only those.
• A path is usable only if the server supports it, the client supports it, the network allows it, the configuration is valid, and authentication and security remain intact.
• Variant ladder per gateway: (1) the user’s profile unchanged; (2) other user-supplied profiles for the same gateway; (3) other user-configured servers; (4) runtime-derived variants only if allowRuntimeVariants is ON (default OFF), limited to options the app already exposes (for example mux, fragmentation or DNS strategy). Derived configs are runtime-only copies, validated by the bundled core’s config parser before use, and never persisted over the original.
• Never try ports, transports or hosts that are not in the configuration. A port or transport change is not assumed to bypass filtering; it is a hypothesis that verification confirms or rejects.
• WireGuard only if it is already integrated; otherwise out of scope.
• Xray’s observatory or balancer may be used only if the bundled version supports it and the audit shows the config builder can enable it safely. App-level logic stays authoritative and the two must not run competing failover.
• Every transport switch is logged (category, from, to, reason) in FailureLog.
14. Authorized relay support
Definition. Android MAXNET5G client → authorized reachable relay → user-owned international gateway → international internet.
Requirements. The relay MUST be explicitly configured, under the operator’s or user’s control or explicitly authorized, authenticated, encrypted, able to forward the required transport, and have a genuine path to the gateway. Relay selection is based on measured reachability and failures are detected. Domestic websites and third-party services are never relays. If no relay or viable alternative exists, report the real limitation.
Client modes (choose per the audit; no second engine):
• A. Relay-fronted profile (preferred). The operator supplies an ordinary profile in an already-supported format whose server address is the relay. It is tagged as a relay path in the path model; no Xray config mutation.
• A2. Address override for a transparent TCP forwarder. A runtime-derived variant equals the gateway profile with address and port replaced by the user-supplied relay address while every identity-bearing parameter is preserved. The effective TLS server name (and the WebSocket Host, if used) MUST remain the gateway’s, so certificate validation still targets the gateway. Never persist it over the original and never disable validation.
• B. Explicit chain (conditional). Only if the audit proves the bundled Xray supports chaining and the config builder can generate it safely; runtime-only, validated by the core’s config parser.
• C. Unsupported. Document the limitation.
Data and secrets. An additive relay model (id, label, endpoint, transport kind, auth reference, enabled). Secrets go in the project’s existing secure storage or Android Keystore-backed encryption. Reject relay entries without authentication and encryption.
Health. Two levels: RELAY_REACHABLE (TCP or TLS reachable) and RELAY_FORWARDING_OK (end-to-end overlay verification through the relay to the gateway succeeded). Only the second counts as usable. Failures yield RELAY_UNAVAILABLE with a TTL. A relay is not preferred over a working direct path unless the direct path is assessed failing or the user prefers it.
UI. Show «از طریق مسیر واسط» only when a relay is configured and in use.
Server-side deliverable: docs/connectivity/RELAY_SETUP.md with topologies (transparent TCP forwarder, Xray-based relay), requirements (authentication, encryption, firewall, health endpoint, logging policy), what the client expects, and an operations checklist. Placeholders only, no credentials. State that the operator is responsible for lawful use.
Tests: a fake relay in JVM tests: relay unreachable; relay reachable but gateway unreachable; relay fully working.
15. Security, privacy and leak prevention
• 15.1 TLS. Platform trust store and hostname verification everywhere. Optional SPKI pinning for the developer’s own probe endpoint is allowed, with a backup pin and a documented rotation plan; default OFF. A pin or certificate failure yields TLS_CERTIFICATE_INVALID (possible interception) and is never ignored.
• 15.2 DNS. While connected, application DNS goes through the tunnel path the app already configures. Underlay probes resolve only registry hostnames. Do not add new underlay resolutions of user hostnames: prefer cached addresses, and resolve the gateway hostname on the underlay only as part of establishing that path, as the core already does.
• 15.3 IPv6. Audit the tunnel’s IPv6 handling. IPv6 traffic must be routed into the tunnel and handled, or explicitly blocked; document and test the result, and record it in the report.
• 15.4 Identifiers and redaction. endpointId is a truncated HMAC-SHA-256 of host and port, keyed with a per-install random secret kept in secure storage. A unit-tested redaction utility removes UUIDs, keys, tokens, passwords, share-link URIs (vless, vmess, trojan and similar), subscription URLs, and the IP addresses and hostnames of user servers from any text before it reaches a log or diagnostic.
• 15.5 Diagnostics export. Sanitized text, only by explicit user action, with a preview and a size cap. No automatic upload. A control to clear records.
• 15.6 Storage and backup. New stores follow the project’s existing backup rules and are excluded from auto-backup wherever the project excludes similar data.
• 15.7 Components. New components are not exported; no new intent filters; PendingIntents are immutable; no secrets on the clipboard.
• 15.8 Relay authentication. Mandatory (14).
• 15.9 Threat model (document in the final report): an ISP or DPI observer; device inspection; a malicious relay; tampered probe responses; DNS poisoning; captive portals. For each, state the mitigation implemented and the residual risk.
16. MaxNetVpnService integration
• Create the engine in the existing start path with scope SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.Default, cancelled in onDestroy and on user disconnect; unregister every callback.
• Single-TUN invariant (11.6). onRevoke() stops the engine and does not auto-reconnect.
• Notification: update the text through the existing builder, only on a user-visible state change and at most every 2 s; no sensitive content.
• Always-on and lockdown: detect with isAlwaysOn() and isLockdownEnabled() where the API level allows and adapt the hint in the advanced section (with an optional shortcut to the system VPN settings screen).
• Doze and standby: no new alarms or wake locks; adaptive cadence; never start services from the background in ways the OS forbids.
• Process death and restart: engine state is reconstructible; use the restart-storm guard (11.7).
• Crash safety: engine orchestration runs under supervised handlers. An unexpected exception disables the engine for the session, logs a sanitized message and leaves the VPN running as before.
• Thread safety: shared state lives in StateFlow or is confined to a single dispatcher; no blocking calls on the main thread.